{
  "topic": "Fractions (types)",
  "affirmation": "You made fractions feel visible and familiar to the class.",
  "identity": "You teach by showing first: you held up circles, shaded parts, and counters so students could see 'parts of a whole' before you asked for words. You circle back to the room again and again—asking 'What is a fraction?' and using the cake story so abstract language landed in everyday life.",
  "moments": [
    {
      "title": "Modeling the whole",
      "quote": "یہ ایک سرکل ہے۔ سرکل ہے، لیکن یہ ایک پورا ہول ہے نا؟",
      "why": "You used a concrete partitioned circle to shift attention from shape to whole-with-parts, which laid the visual foundation for fractions."
    },
    {
      "title": "Asking for definition",
      "quote": "تو یہ کیا کہلاتا ہے؟ یہ فیکشن۔ فیکشن کیا ہے؟ واٹ از فیکشن؟",
      "why": "You paused and pressed for a definition, which exposed where students' procedural recall was thinner than conceptual understanding."
    },
    {
      "title": "Real-life cake link",
      "quote": "مثلاً آپ اس کو سمجھیں یہ کیک ہے۔ کیک پسند ہے نا سب کو؟ یس۔ جب آپ کیک لاتی ہیں اپنی مما، تو آپ اس کو کیا کرتی ہیں؟ پورا کھا جاتی ہیں ایسے؟ نو۔ نہیں کرتے۔ ہم پارٹ کرتے ہیں۔",
      "why": "You anchored the idea in a shared, everyday moment so students could see why equal parts matter, making math social and meaningful."
    }
  ],
  "strength_name": "Concrete visual models",
  "strength_note": "You consistently used circles, rectangles, shaded areas and colored counters to make the language of numerator and denominator tangible.",
  "horizon_title": "Specific corrective feedback",
  "horizon_note": "Turning quick confirmations into short, concrete fixes—for example, naming the exact counting mistake and giving a one-step correction—would have turned many right/wrong checks into clear learning moves.",
  "journey_note": "In this single lesson you guided students from naming shapes to saying 'parts of a whole,' and the observed indicators peaked at 43% while the classroom repeatedly returned to the core idea.",
  "score_framing": "A 43% snapshot is a clear stage, not a verdict—you already showed high-value moves to sharpen next time.",
  "domain_whys": {
    "high_leverage_practices": "This is developing because you used class choruses and named numerator and denominator aloud when students repeated them — it's not full marks because you did not give specific, actionable feedback to individual students on how to correct their mistakes.",
    "student_engagement": "This is developing because you grounded the concept in everyday talk (the cake example) and invited whole-class responses — it's not full marks because students did not offer explanatory reasons or spontaneous connections in their answers.",
    "teacher_subject_knowledge": "This is developing because you modeled fraction notation, labeled numerator and denominator, and corrected a child who miscounted the total — it's not full marks because you did not explicitly name and confront the misconception that students were counting shaded parts as the whole."
  }
}
